(name * in #genitive) iškišaš
(name * in #dative) iškiši
(name * in #ablative) iškišaz
(* has modifier #watchtower)
(from * go #south to #gatehouse)
(from * go #up to #roof)
(from * go #north to #enemyland)

(prevent [[go #roof] $Details])
	(current room *)
	~(#ladder is #in *)
	(describe failed action [[cross #roof] $Details])
	Nu šuḫḫa parku ēšta. %% "The roof is high up"

(prevent [[go #roof] $Details])
	(current player $Player)
	(#amphora is #heldby $Player)
	(describe failed action [[cross #roof] $Details])
	Nu palḫiš nakkiš ēšta. %% "The amphora is heavy."

#roof
(room *)
(neuter *)
(* uses preverb #on)
(name * in #nominative/#accusative) šuḫḫan
(name * in #genitive) šuḫḫaš
(name * in #dative) šuḫḫi
(name * in #ablative) šuḫḫaz
(name * in #bare) šuḫḫa
(from * go #down to #backroom)
(from * go #north to #precinct)

#precinct
(room *)
(neuter *)
(name * in #nominative/#accusative/#dative/#bare) ḫali
(name * in #genitive) ḫaliyaš
(name * in #ablative) ḫaliyaz
(* has modifier @karimnaš)
(dict * in $) karimnac

%% For later: "temple" is É^karimmi, neuter: karimmi, karimnac, karimni, karimnaz

(instead of [[go *] $Details])
	(current player $Player)
	(if) (#cup is #heldby $Player) (cup has water) (then)
		(describe action [[go *] $Details])
		(div @narration) { The tablet breaks off here, as the soldier finally enters the temple precinct. }
		(game over { To be continued... } )
	(else)
		(describe failed action [[go *] $Details])
		Nu=za šaknuwanza ešun. Nu wātar natta ḫarkun. %% "I was impure. I did not have water."
	(endif)

#enemyland
(room *)
(* uses preverb #at)
(short name *) KÚR
(name * in #bare) URU LÚ KÚR
(logogram *)
(dict * in $) LU2 KUR2

(prevent [[go *] | $Details])
	(describe failed action [[go *] | $Details])
	%% Basic failure message should be enough, given the definitions of the words

(parse metacommand [\/test watchtower])
	(now) (#ladder is #in #corner)
	(now) (#ladder is handled)
	(now) (#street is visited)
	(now) (#bolt is pristine)
	(now) (#bolt is #in #gate)
	(enter #gatehouse)

(parse metacommand [\/test roof])
	(now) (#bolt is #heldby #me)
	(now) (bolt is burning)
	(now) (amphora has water)

%% Tutorial!

(tutorial)
	~(given first message)
	(div @tutorial) {
		Welcome to (span @title) { Ḫattušumna Laḫḫa }! This game is presented entirely in the ancient Hittite language, which is probably a bit intimidating at first. But don't panic; just take it slow. (par)
		Clicking a word will show you its definition. See if you can use that to figure out the meaning of the two sentences at the top left. When you think you understand them, press ↩ to continue.
	}
	(now) (given first message)
	(now) (update needed) %% Redraw the status bar to include verbs next time

(tutorial)
	~(taught syntax)
	(div @tutorial) {
		You probably came up with something like "I was at the riverbank. I saw a river there." Most Hittite sentences start with a meaningless "connective" word, then have a series of nouns, and finish off with a verb. The (span @italic) case of those nouns indicates their purpose: a "dative" noun like (span @hittite) wappi indicates a location or destination, while an "accusative" noun like (span @hittite) ḫapan indicates the object of the verb.
		(if) (#watchtower is unvisited) (then)
			(par) Now it's time to construct a sentence of your own. The compass rose at the top right indicates nearby locations, and the "verbs" bar suggests some relevant verbs. Double-click any of these words to insert them into your sentence; see if you can use them to move to a new location.
			(now) (prompted movement specifically)
		(endif)
	}
	(now) (taught syntax)

(tutorial)
	~(taught objects)
	(#watchtower is visited)
	(div @tutorial) {
		(if) (prompted movement specifically) (then)
			There we go!
		(endif)
		You'll encounter many more verbs and nouns as you go along, but all the relevant ones will be shown at the top of the screen: what you can see and where you can go. \( You can also leave off the sentence connectives if you want. \)
	}
	(now) (taught objects)

(tutorial)
	~(taught determinatives)
	(#gatehouse is visited)
	(div @tutorial) {
		Hittite nouns were sometimes written with a special sign in front, called a "determinative". These weren't pronounced, but indicated something about the noun, like how "amber" is a rock but "Amber" is a person. GIŠ, for example, marks that something is made of wood.
	}
	(now) (taught determinatives)

(tutorial)
	~(taught heterograms)
	(heterograms used)
	(div @tutorial) {
		Certain cuneiform signs were used as shorthand for entire words, like how "7" is short for "seven", or "&" short for "and". These signs are written in CAPITALS to make it clear that they're not conveying the actual Hittite pronunciation.
	}
	(now) (taught heterograms)

(on every tick) %% Make sure this comes BEFORE redrawing the status bar
	~(direction numbers enabled)
	(current room #watchtower)
	{ (#ladder is handled) (or) (#corner is visited) }
	(now) (direction numbers enabled)

(tutorial)
	~(taught numbers)
	(direction numbers enabled)
	(div @tutorial) {
		You're getting the hang of this now! Since you'll be moving around a lot in this game, there's a shorter way to get from place to place: the Babylonians sometimes numbered their compass directions, so you can type "1 3 2" to go south, then east, then north. These numbers will be shown on the compass rose in the top right. (par)
		\( There's no evidence that the Hittites actually did this, and adding numbers for "up" and "down" is completely non-historical. But the Hittites imitating Babylonian writing isn't much of a stretch, and typing a full sentence every time gets tedious very fast. \)
	}
	(now) (taught numbers)

(tutorial)
	~(taught multiple nouns)
	(#bolt is handled)
	(#foyer is visited)
	(div @tutorial) {
		Most of the sentences you've seen so far have only one noun, but this doesn't have to be the case. To put one thing into or onto another, for example, you'll need both an accusative noun (span @italic) and a dative noun.
	}
	(now) (taught multiple nouns)

(tutorial) %% Don't fail
